#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n2. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n2. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A$, given below?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry.\n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n-1+(-4)+8=3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d1edd0-3945-591f-b469-0c56a6f221e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128049} {:answer/id #uuid "9f253548-73aa-51fb-82d4-29968d728888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a_{22}$ and $a_{33}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128050} {:answer/id #uuid "423951bc-517e-58c5-803b-bd57dd94a2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a_{21}$, $a_{33}$, and $a_{44}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128051} {:answer/id #uuid "66b741ec-8de8-59ac-b4f5-7d5c74b02817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a_{21}$ and $a_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128052} {:answer/id #uuid "75771f0c-ca49-5518-8b80-3f9a0f0a50cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a_{21}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66b741ec-8de8-59ac-b4f5-7d5c74b02817" :answer/type {:db/ident :ans